hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well early on in  Chapter six of his language truth and  logic a jair is going to provide us with  a breakdown of what he takes to be going  on in some sort of systematic account of  ethics so when we think about moral  theories for example or the works in  which they are articulated whether these  be presentations or books or articles  you know or as he says the ordinary  system of ethics as elaborated in the  works of ethical philosophers he's going  to tell us that this consists of a  number of different types of things or  classes of components or statements if  you like and he tells us these are not  homogeneous that means that they have a  number of different functions or they  have different natures to themselves and  before we look at the four that he picks  out as being ethical in some sense it's  also worth noting that he tells us that  very often systems of ethics contain  pieces of metaphysics analyses of non  ethical concepts if we want to think  about things like a platonic dialogue it  might also include  you know all sorts of character  development and setting all of that we  want to push aside and say okay what's  the actual ethical content of some sort  of expression some sort of systematic  articulation of how it is that we human  beings ought to behave what's good and  bad what's right and wrong and how we  should understand all of these manners  so air will tell us that there's really  four main things going on and we could  distinguish them from each other we  could pull them apart and you know think  about taking a book for example like his  own and maybe we have four different  colors of highlighter and every time we  see something going on with you know the  first class we highlighted as light blue  and then yellow and green and finally  maybe pink for the other class or we  could do so in in many other ways as  well maybe we could even like break the  text down and say let's take all of  class one and put it over here all of  class three and put it over here and  thereby develop new texts so what are  these he tells us that the we may divide  them into four main classes there are  first of all propositions which Express  definitions of ethical terms or  judgments about the legitimacy or  possibility of certain definitions  sounds rather abstract but that's  actually you know the part that air is  most interested in as it's going to turn  out Lee says then secondly there are  propositions describing the phenomena of  moral experience and their causes so  we'll come back to that in just a moment  because you might be wondering well what  are phenomena of moral experience let  alone their causes third he says there  are exhortation to moral virtue and what  does an exhortation and expectation is  saying that somebody ought to do  something you're trying to convince them  or trying to stir them up to do  something so when I tell a student for  example you know  was anxious about the exam I said just  do your best right  I'm exhorting them you're probably not  gonna get everything right but just try  your hardest I'm trying to produce some  sort of action on their part albeit in  this case an action that's at a second  level you know an action about their own  actions use your Faculty of will to make  yourself do things this way or look on  the bright side or anything like that  now an exhortation of moral virtue would  be saying you should do something that  we take as being good or don't do  something that we take as being bad and  then finally he says we also have actual  ethical judgments so let's come back to  the first class again propositions which  Express definitions of ethical terms or  judgments about the legitimacy or  possibility of certain definitions so  you know we not only have definitions we  don't just throw them out there we say  is this a good definition does this  actually work can we define something  like this we can go on further and have  as he says comments on definitions may  be we require some explanation for that  and interestingly enough he tells us  that this first part so he says it's  easy to see that only the first of our  four the our four classes namely that  which comprises the propositions  relating to the definitions of ethical  terms can be said to constitute ethical  philosophy so the only thing that really  should strictly speaking count as ethics  itself in terms of ethical philosophy  would be this first class what about the  second class interestingly air is going  to push that off to what at that time  were the premier social sciences now we  have many many other social sciences and  a lot of overlap between those things  and here he's talking about he calls it  propositions describing phenomena of  moral experience so  nomina are the things that appear to us  the things that we have deserve right  and moral experience would be that  entire realm of things that we either we  directly observed or somebody else  observed or could observe that have to  do with some sort of value so when we  say for example World War two resulted  in horrible loss of life but it was  quite important to fight totalitarianism  even if we also had to cooperate with  another totalitarian regime that is the  Soviet Russians we are we are engaging  not just in some sort of you know  definition process but we're describing  moral phenomenon we could say you know  what is it like to actually have to kill  another human being is that a bad thing  is that a good thing  does this thinking about it in terms of  doing your duty or saving civilians or  preventing a much worse outcome or just  being brave does that help you in any  way these are things that we would call  moral phenomena and air thinks that  philosophy really can't be concerned  with this so this means that a lot of  things that we find in older ethics air  would say that's not really ethics  that's for the scientists to determine  and which scientists in his case he  talks about the psychologists and the  sociologists psychologists focusing on  the individual or the individual and a  small context sociologists focusing on  groups we might include economists we  might to some degree include historians  we could include studies of  communication anthropology all sorts of  other things but the point is here air  thinks that any of this stuff in class  two really doesn't belong in philosophy  not even in that branch of philosophy  that we call ethics going on to the the  third part exhortation to moral virtue  err points out that these aren't really  prappas  at all they might express themselves in  in the form of pseudo form of  propositions like it is highly desirable  that you complete your work on time and  diligently write if I write something  like that I'm really not just saying I'm  telling you what happens to be highly  desirable it's up to you what you want  to do no I'm telling you I want you to  complete your work on time and  diligently do that because I think  that's good I'm going to give you a you  know rah rah if you do that and I'm  gonna give you a boo if you do otherwise  right these are not propositions at all  as we've said and expectations could be  to all sorts of things you know if the  word moral virtue is tripping you up  just think about exportations commands  injunctions attempts to convince people  of something maybe even salesmanship  might fall in there you really can't  deprive yourself of this new  refrigerator that's probably an  exhortation right it is using some what  ethical language you you know you've got  to exercise your right to be a good  consumer it's something along those  lines that's really in that third part  then we have actual ethical judgments  and at that point in the work when he's  going through these these four types he  says as for the expressions of ethical  judgments we have not yet determined how  they should be classified but in as much  as they are certainly neither  definitions and or comments on  definition so they're not the first part  nor quotations we may say decisively  they do not belong to ethical philosophy  so insofar as they're not involving some  sort of definition they're not really  part of ethics a little bit later error  is going to set out an analysis of  ethical judgments where he'll say they  they often have some factual components  those we could actually like you know  reasonably disagree about and have  decision procedures for figuring out  but they also have ethical components to  them ethical terminology and that is  really just an expression of our  emotions or it's an attempt to get other  people to feel the same way that we do  or to induce some sort of action on  their part that leads into the emotive  theory of ethics that he develops there  so coming back to this air is making  this distinction why in part because he  says that unfortunately the distinction  between these four classes plain as it  is is commonly ignored by ethical  philosophers with the results it's often  very difficult to tell from their works  what it is they are seeking to discover  or approve heir says that if we're not  distinguishing betwe